set  or anything he i guess he never actually  knew what happened afterwards  orelli says this accidental kidnapping  allowed them to talk with the mayor  one-on-one  and later she found that combining this  ground-level direct action with talking  to politicians it really paid off  mexico city started installing the bike  lanes that it promised  20 years ago there was nothing there was  not one meter of bike lanes and today we  have 270 kilometers  that's about 170 miles an hour hopes  that this could be the beginning of  mexico city becoming a bike mecca  and if you think that's a pipe dream  get ready  we're about to travel to a country  that's a cyclist's dream and they got  there with the help of their own  vigilantes  more after this  a city with more bikes than cars might  sound like a fantasy  but it's already happened  today the netherlands is known as the  biking capital of the world  dutch streets are filled with bikes they  roll through some of the smoothest and  most connected bike paths in the world  here's john poocher again people have  this image i think americans have an  image oh cycling is just paradise and  it's always been paradise in europe in  germany and the netherlands and denmark  wrong  not true  in the 60s and 70s the netherlands was  like the us dominated by cars  the dutch economy was booming after  world war ii and more and more people  started buying cars  more traffic meant more crashes  in 1972 more than 450 children died in  traffic accidents  from all european countries the  netherlands had the highest level of  children being killed in road accidents  that's mario landalana a mobility expert  and a cyclist from the netherlands  when she was a kid she'd hear about  these accidents  and we heard that this girl  was killed  in  in a car accident  just a couple of blocks away on a main  road where i have had been cycling  every now and then so  it was quite  intense  and another girl she was killed on a  road between two  villages and then it happened to a  newspaper reporter  his daughter was killed on a bike on her  way to school in a traffic accident and  a year later another daughter was nearly  killed  and he said we have to go and do  something about it so his newspaper  printed a front page editorial about the  traffic deaths titled stop the child  murder  a community of activists grew around  this movement they demanded safer  streets and better infrastructure for  cyclists and pedestrians  one such activist was tom crotifroy he's  a visual artist and urban planning  mobility expert from the netherlands  well back then the car was the king of  the road so the city was packed with  cars  cars were driving everywhere  so in the early 80s tom and some other  activists wanted a bike lane on this  particularly tricky one-way street  so at some point we  send a level to the municipality that  that if they wouldn't do it we would do  it  so one dark night tom and his fellow  cyclist friends snuck out onto the  street and started painting the lane  themselves  just as they were finishing up two  police cars rolled up  and they put us all in the car to take  us to the police station  but the police didn't seem too angry  well  they said we had fair done it very uh  nicely  it looked well at the police station  they said that yeah yeah yeah the police  held them in custody for a few hours and  let them go but the most funny part was  that one of the shopkeepers in that time  the next morning noticed the cycle lane  in front of his shop the shopkeeper got  mad because he thought the lane should  be a space for parking not bikes  and then this funny thing happened  so he started to remove our  lame  and then he was fined by the police by  doing illegal work on the street  the newspapers wrote about the lane for  a week  the city finally caved to public  pressure and decided to keep it the  municipality claimed that they had  wanted to do it themselves we then send  a bill to the municipality for having  done the job for them  tom and activists around the country got  what they wanted in those days the the  shopping streets were packed with cars  and now there's hardly any  so  nowadays there is no need to go  painting bicycle lanes in the night  because it has been done by the  municipalities  thanks to all these movements these days  the netherlands has more than 23 000  miles of bike paths  when you walk through a dutch city you  see streets filled with bikes not cars  and as for traffic deaths  tom says that in the mid 70s over 3000  people were dying in traffic accidents a  year  that was in a population of 12 million  so that was quite high and  now the number of fatalities each year  is  with a grown population which is now 18  million  we are now at about 500 a year  i heard people say that a bike  revolution like this could only happen  in the netherlands  but this can happen and is happening  around the world  london paris tel aviv beijing they're  all rolling out bike networks  and if you think the us will never get  there  again john pooker  and so here in the united states i find  so many people  who are are disappointed or they're  impatient um and they say oh it's never  going to work in the united states well  yeah we've just started the us has only  been building out bike lanes and other  bike infrastructure for the last 20  years or so  but we're ramping up big time  most cities are making it way easier to  bike around  new york los angeles portland and 50 or  60 other american cities are building  out their bike networks  in fact if you live in a city there's a  good chance this is happening near you  i notice new bike lanes popping up  around chicago all the time  it's the kind of thing that's invisible  until you start looking for it and then  you see it everywhere  [Music]  and so we're sort of at the beginning  stage of where europe was maybe five  decades ago and it's going to take us a  while to get really a broad spectrum of  society cycling or it could happen fast  like look at the spanish city of seville  the government there rolled out a whole  bike network in less than two years  now the city has over 50 miles of  protected bike lanes and cycling has  increased over 11 times  aurelie believes quick change like this  could happen in north america too  she says we're moving away from the car  century and into a new era a world that  isn't dominated by the fear of getting  hit with cars so  i'm happy to say that this other world  is existing already and it's coming  and  it's no point in resisting and denying  that it's happening and  it's not you know only for a woman's  sake or or for the earth's sake or for  the children's secrets everybody needs a  better  planet a better way of living  and we have to choose to do that and  it's one of the tools that is very cheap  and it's maybe in your  garage right now it's a bicycle so  go  use it  enjoy it  more after this  [Music]  there is so much ground we couldn't  cover in our episode if you want to dive  even deeper into the world of green  transportation subscribers can check out  our september issue of the magazine  which features stories about electric  cars and hydrogen powered planes  also if you're interested in keeping up  with bike enthusiasts in our episode you  can follow urban planner jorge kanyes on  twitter at payatonito  learn more about aurelie carrion's work  at busytecas.org  and finally you can read more of john  puker's research in his book cycling for  sustainable cities that's all in your  show notes  and if you like overheard please take a  minute to rate and review us it's a big  help  [Music]  overheard at national geographic is  produced by laura sim brian gutierrez  jacob pinter and me alana strauss  our senior editor is eli chen  our senior producer is carla wills  our executive producer of audio is  devara ardellon  our fact-checkers are robin palmer and  julie beer our copy editor is amy  kolczak  and hans del su composed our theme music  and also sound design and engineer this  episode  this podcast is a production of national  geographic partners  whitney johnson is the director of  visuals and immersive experiences  susan goldberg is national geographic's  editorial director  and i'm your host ilana strauss  thanks for listening and see y'all next  time  [Music]  you
 

